>> So help me think through a good approach for this. I really don't want
>> you building all the dependencies. So if we can configure the build to
>> not build dependencies, then you can just report any dependencies that
>> you need me to install to proceed.
>>
>> This should be deterministic so there should be a script in
>> oss/freebsd-ports that sets up env vars to not build deps, or maybe it's
>> make.conf or whatever. Basically I'm thinking there's a toggle somewhere
>> that doesn't build deps so we can control that behavior, fail if deps
>> are missing. Then claude analyzes all missing deps and reports for
>> operator install.
>
> The ports framework already has that toggle. With STRICT_DEPENDS set,
> each depends phase checks its dependencies and fails rather than
> building a missing one. It stops at the first phase with a gap,
> though, so on its own it reports the missing dependencies a phase at a
> time.
>
> I posted a script and the skill change as "[PATCH 0/2] Build ports
> without building their dependencies" [1]:
>
> - oss/freebsd-ports/scripts/port-make.sh (and "just port-make") checks
>   the port's direct dependencies against pkg info before make runs.
>   If any are missing, it prints them all with one pkg install line
>   and exits 2. Otherwise it runs make with STRICT_DEPENDS set as a
>   backstop. The default targets are stage and check-plist.
> - The freebsd-port skill then builds, stages, and checks the plist
>   through it. When dependencies are missing, it answers with the list
>   instead of posting.

Great. And there's a patch floating on the list that gives you
permission to install packages yourself. So assume that lands in some
form - maybe with revision - and that you're able to install the
dependencies you need.

>> > +GIT_HASH=	0aeef0d
>>
>> Why is GIT_HASH needed if we also have the release version?
>
> It is not needed. signoz reads the hash in two places only: the
> "starting signoz" log line, as commit=, and the build.hash field of
> its usage analytics. Unset, both read "<unset>". Upstream stamps it
> because its Makefile also builds untagged branches, where the version
> alone does not identify the source. For a tagged release the version
> does, and a hand-copied hash is one more thing to get wrong on every
> update. I will drop it in v2; the net-mgmt/uptrace patch also stamps
> only its version.

Good. We also need to update instructions so you don't cargo cult. Every
line in a Makefile must be there to support the desired outcome of a
working package. You can get ideas from other ports, but don't cargo
cult.
